 To add IDs to the HTML elements in your code, you can use the `htmlFor` attribute or just simply append an `id` attribute to each element. Here's how you can modify your code to include IDs for better accessibility and ease of manipulation:

```jsx
export const CreateObjectView = observer(({ model }: { model: CreateObjectModel }) => {
  return (
    <div id="CreateObjectView">
      <label htmlFor="asset-select">Asset:</label>
      <Select
        id="asset-select"
        value={model.selectedAsset}
        onChange={(e) => {
          if (e.target.value) {
            model.createObject(e.target.value).then();
            model.setSelectedAsset(e.target.value);
          }
        }}
      >
        {model.availableAssets.map((asset, index) => (
          <option key={asset.id} value={asset.id}>
            {asset.name}
          </option>
        ))}
      </Select>
      <button id="clean-scene-btn" onClick={() => model.cleanupScene()}>Cleanup scene</button>
    </div>
  );
});
```

In this example, I added IDs to the container div ("CreateObjectView"), the select input ("asset-select"), and the cleanup scene button ("clean-scene-btn"). The asset options also received unique keys based on their index within the array. This makes it easier to target these elements with CSS or when writing tests using tools like Jest.

